      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "An epidemic of Shigella dysenteriae type I is spreading through Africa. It is a particularly infectious and virulent form of dysentery which can cause clinical confusion with other endemic diseases and may present to the surgeon as a result of its complications. A total of 140 children with Shigella dysenteriae type I presented to the paediatricians at King Edward VIII Hospital in 1995; 35 were referred to the surgeons because of abdominal tenderness, distension, peritonitis or perforation. Ten children underwent laparotomy--four for peritonitis and six for perforation. Of the four children with peritonitis, three had transmural colitis. Therefore laparotomy was only performed for objective evidence of perforation. Of the subsequent non-operated group with the clinical features of peritonitis, none developed further surgical problems in the acute phase and none died. It is suggested that surgery in the acute phase should be avoided unless there is evidence of perforation."},
